# ---- 001 ----
U["jbf-debug-log-levels-001"] = dict(
    layout_type="concept-explainer",
    direct_answer="Java logging has **five severity levels**, from least to most severe: **TRACE**, **DEBUG**, **INFO**, **WARN**, **ERROR**. The configured level is a **threshold** — set INFO and you see INFO, WARN, and ERROR but not DEBUG or TRACE. TRACE/DEBUG are development diagnostics, INFO is business events, WARN is recoverable surprises, ERROR is real failures.",
    interviewer_intent={
        "testing": "Whether you know the five levels in the correct severity order AND understand that a level is an inclusive threshold, not a single filter.",
        "common_mistake": "Listing the names but saying 'INFO shows only INFO' or reversing the order so ERROR looks more verbose than DEBUG.",
        "to_stand_out": "Tie each level to a production decision: INFO for the business narrative, WARN for handled surprises, ERROR for things that page on-call, and note that framework loggers usually run at WARN while your app runs at INFO.",
    },
    sections=[
        S("overview", "The Five Java Log Levels",
          "Log levels are **severity filters** for your application's output. Java's logging APIs (SLF4J, Logback, Log4j2, `java.util.logging`) all share the same idea: every log statement is tagged with a level, and the logger only emits statements at or above the configured **threshold**.\n\nFrom least to most severe the five standard levels are **TRACE**, **DEBUG**, **INFO**, **WARN**, **ERROR**. The key fresher insight: the level you configure is a *minimum*, not an exact match. Setting INFO means \"INFO and everything more severe\" — so INFO, WARN, and ERROR all appear, while DEBUG and TRACE are suppressed. This lets you turn verbosity up or down with one setting and no code change."),
        S("concept_map", "Five Levels at a Glance",
          "cyan|TRACE|~Finest detail|method entry/exit|almost never in prod|noisiest level\nblue|DEBUG|~Dev diagnostics|SQL, variable values|off in production|for developers only\nemerald|INFO|~Business events|order placed, user registered|default prod level|the app's narrative\namber|WARN|~Handled surprise|retry, fallback used|reviewed, not paged|degraded but working\nrose|ERROR|~Real failure|DB down, unhandled exception|pages on-call|operation failed"),
        S("comparison_table", "What Each Level Is For",
          "| Level | Meaning | Example | On in production? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **TRACE** | Finest-grained tracing | `Entering processOrder(42)` | No |\n| **DEBUG** | Development diagnostics | `Fetched 3 rows for orderId=42` | No |\n| **INFO** | Significant business event | `Order 42 placed for customer 7` | Yes |\n| **WARN** | Unexpected but handled | `Config missing, using default timeout` | Yes |\n| **ERROR** | Failure that stopped the operation | `Payment gateway timeout for order 42` | Yes |\n\nSeverity order: `TRACE < DEBUG < INFO < WARN < ERROR`. The threshold includes everything to its right."),
        S("code_example", "Using Each Level with SLF4J",
          "```java\nimport org.slf4j.Logger;\nimport org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;\n\npublic class OrderService {\n    // One logger per class, named after the class\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderService.class);\n\n    public void processOrder(long orderId) {\n        log.trace(\"Entering processOrder, orderId={}\", orderId);   // finest detail\n        log.debug(\"Loading order from DB, orderId={}\", orderId);    // dev diagnostics\n        Order order = findOrder(orderId);\n        log.info(\"Processing order {} for customer {}\", orderId, order.customerId()); // business event\n        if (order.total() > 10_000) {\n            log.warn(\"Large order {} exceeds threshold: {}\", orderId, order.total()); // handled surprise\n        }\n        try {\n            charge(order);\n        } catch (PaymentException e) {\n            log.error(\"Payment failed for order {}\", orderId, e); // real failure + stack trace\n            throw e;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWith the logger set to INFO, the `trace` and `debug` lines never print — but the same code shows them the moment you lower the threshold to DEBUG."),
        S("common_mistakes", "Common Mistakes",
          "- **Treating levels as independent**: thinking INFO shows only INFO. It's a threshold — INFO also shows WARN and ERROR.\n- **Reversing the order**: assuming ERROR is verbose. ERROR is the *least* verbose; it shows the fewest messages.\n- **Logging everything at one level**: putting framework loggers and your app at the same level, so either you drown in Spring/Hibernate noise or miss your own business events."),
        S("key_points", "Key Points",
          "- **Five levels**: TRACE < DEBUG < INFO < WARN < ERROR (least to most severe).\n- **Threshold, not filter**: setting INFO shows INFO, WARN, and ERROR; hides DEBUG and TRACE.\n- **TRACE/DEBUG** = developer diagnostics, off in production.\n- **INFO** = business narrative; **WARN** = handled surprise; **ERROR** = failure that pages on-call.\n- **Production pattern**: framework loggers at WARN, your application at INFO."),
        S("speakable_answer", "How to Answer This Verbally",
          "Java logging has five standard levels, and the order matters: from least to most severe they're TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, and ERROR. The thing most freshers get wrong is thinking the level you set is an exact filter. It isn't — it's a threshold. If I set my logger to INFO, I see INFO and everything more severe, so INFO, WARN, and ERROR all show up, but DEBUG and TRACE get filtered out. Let me walk through what each one is for. TRACE is the finest detail, like method entry and exit — you almost never turn it on, even in development. DEBUG is for developer diagnostics, like the SQL that just ran or the value of a variable; it's on locally and off in production. INFO is the business narrative — an order was placed, a user registered, a job finished. That's what you keep on in production. WARN is for something unexpected that you handled gracefully, like falling back to a default config or a retry that eventually succeeded — worth noticing, but the app kept working. ERROR is a real failure, like the database being down or an unhandled exception — that's the kind of thing that should page someone. A detail that makes you sound experienced: in production you usually set framework loggers like Spring or Hibernate to WARN so they stay quiet, and keep your own application code at INFO so you can actually follow what it's doing."),
    ],
    hook="Most people can list the five level names — the real test is knowing a level is a threshold, so INFO quietly includes WARN and ERROR too.",
)

# ---- 002 ----
U["jbf-debug-log-levels-002"] = dict(
    layout_type="recipe-builder",
    direct_answer="Pick the level by asking **who needs to see this, and how urgently**. **ERROR** = an operation failed and someone should act now. **WARN** = unexpected but handled (retry, fallback). **INFO** = a normal business event worth keeping in production. **DEBUG** = developer-only diagnostics. The classic trap: logging expected exceptions like validation failures at ERROR, which creates alert fatigue.",
    interviewer_intent={
        "testing": "Practical judgment — can you map a real situation to the right level instead of defaulting everything to ERROR or DEBUG.",
        "common_mistake": "Logging every caught exception at ERROR, including expected ones like a declined card or invalid input, so real failures drown in noise.",
        "to_stand_out": "Distinguish expected business exceptions (user typo, declined card) from genuine system failures, and mention alert fatigue as the operational cost of over-using ERROR.",
    },
    sections=[
        S("overview", "A Decision Framework for Log Levels",
          "Choosing a level comes down to one question: **who needs to see this message, and when?** If on-call should be paged at 3 AM, it's **ERROR**. If it should be reviewed during business hours, it's **WARN**. If it describes normal business flow, it's **INFO**. If only a developer debugging locally needs it, it's **DEBUG**.\n\nThe distinction freshers miss is **expected vs unexpected**. A user entering an invalid email is expected — that's not an ERROR. A database connection dropping is unexpected — that is. Your level choice directly shapes alert quality and on-call sanity."),
        S("flow_diagram", "Picking the Level",
          "```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    A[New log statement] --> B{Did the operation<br/>fail to complete?}\n    B -->|Yes, system fault| C[ERROR<br/>pages on-call]\n    B -->|No, but handled<br/>a surprise| D[WARN]\n    B -->|No, normal flow| E{Useful in<br/>production?}\n    E -->|Yes, business event| F[INFO]\n    E -->|No, dev diagnostics| G[DEBUG]\n```"),
        S("step", "Step 1 — Did the Operation Fail?",
          "Ask whether the situation stopped the caller's request from completing.\n\n| Situation | Failed? | Level |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payment gateway is down | Yes, system fault | ERROR |\n| Card declined by bank | No, user retries | INFO/DEBUG |\n| Retry succeeded after one timeout | No, handled | WARN |\n| Order saved successfully | No, normal | INFO |\n\nIf you are catching an exception, ask: is this something I *anticipated* in business logic, or a genuine system problem?"),
        S("code_example", "Step 2 — Match the Audience to the Level",
          "```java\ntry {\n    paymentGateway.charge(order);\n} catch (CardDeclinedException e) {\n    // Expected business outcome — the user will try another card\n    log.info(\"Card declined for order {}: {}\", order.id(), e.reasonCode());\n} catch (GatewayTimeoutException e) {\n    // Unexpected but we recovered via retry\n    log.warn(\"Gateway slow for order {}, retrying\", order.id());\n} catch (GatewayDownException e) {\n    // System failure — the order cannot be placed\n    log.error(\"Payment gateway unavailable for order {}\", order.id(), e);\n    throw e;\n}\n```\n\nThree exceptions, three very different levels — because the *consequence* differs, not because they are all 'errors'."),
        S("common_mistakes", "Common Mistakes",
          "- **Every exception is ERROR**: catching a validation or authentication failure and logging it at ERROR, paging people for normal user behavior.\n- **Detail at INFO**: putting cache hits or query timings at INFO, making production logs unreadable. That's DEBUG.\n- **Choosing for local dev**: picking levels by what's convenient on your laptop instead of what production observability needs."),
        S("key_points", "Key Points",
          "- **ERROR** = operation failed, needs action now (pages on-call).\n- **WARN** = unexpected but handled (retry, fallback, soft limit).\n- **INFO** = normal business event worth keeping in production.\n- **DEBUG** = developer diagnostics, off in production.\n- **Expected ≠ ERROR**: invalid input and declined cards are normal, not failures.\n- **Over-using ERROR causes alert fatigue** — real failures get ignored."),
        S("speakable_answer", "How to Answer This Verbally",
          "When I add a log statement, I decide the level by asking who needs to see this and how urgently. My first question is: did this situation actually stop the operation from completing? If the database is down and I can't save an order, that's ERROR, because the operation failed and someone needs to act. But if a user typed an invalid email and validation caught it, that's not an error at all — that's expected behavior, so it's INFO or even DEBUG. The user will just fix it and resubmit. My second question is about production visibility. Significant business events like an order being placed or a user registering go at INFO, because that's the narrative operations teams follow. Something unexpected that I handled — say I retried a slow call and it eventually worked, or I fell back to a default config — that's WARN. It's worth noticing, but the app kept working. Anything that's purely developer diagnostics, like the exact SQL or a cache hit, goes at DEBUG and stays off in production. The biggest mistake I see freshers make is logging every caught exception at ERROR. Not every exception is a failure. A declined card or a wrong password is normal user behavior. If you log those at ERROR, you get alerts constantly, and that's alert fatigue — eventually people ignore the alerts, and when a real database outage happens, it gets missed in the noise. So my rule is simple: ERROR for things that broke and need attention, WARN for handled surprises, INFO for normal business activity, DEBUG for developer detail."),
    ],
    hook="The trick to picking a log level isn't the definitions — it's separating a real failure from a user simply doing something you already planned for.",
)

