U["jbf-debug-log-levels-003"] = dict(
    layout_type="recipe-builder",
    direct_answer="In Spring Boot you set per-package levels in **application.properties** (or YAML) with the `logging.level.<package>` prefix. Keep `logging.level.root=INFO` as a safe default, then raise just your own code with `logging.level.com.myapp=DEBUG`. You can target a single class, suppress framework noise, and even change levels at runtime via **Spring Boot Actuator** without a restart.",
    interviewer_intent={
        "testing": "Whether you know the concrete Spring Boot mechanism (logging.level.<package>) and the root-vs-package strategy, not just that 'you can configure logging'.",
        "common_mistake": "Setting the root logger to DEBUG to debug your own code, which floods logs with Spring, Hibernate, and Tomcat internals.",
        "to_stand_out": "Mention the Actuator /loggers endpoint for changing a level live during an incident, plus per-profile config for dev vs prod.",
    },
    sections=[
        S("overview", "Per-Package Logging in Spring Boot",
          "Different parts of an app need different verbosity. Framework code (Spring, Hibernate, Tomcat) emits huge amounts of DEBUG that would bury your own logs, so you keep the **root logger** conservative and selectively raise your own packages.\n\nSpring Boot reads this from **application.properties** / **application.yml** using the `logging.level` prefix. Logger names follow the Java package hierarchy, so `logging.level.com.myapp` controls every class under that package — surgical control with zero code changes."),
        S("concept_map", "Where Levels Are Set",
          "blue|root logger|~Default for everything|logging.level.root=INFO|covers all libraries|keep it conservative\nemerald|your package|~Your own code|logging.level.com.app=DEBUG|raise while investigating|children inherit\namber|single class|~Targeted deep dive|logging.level.com.app.X=TRACE|one class only|finest control\nviolet|runtime|~No restart|Actuator /loggers POST|flip during incident|set back after"),
        S("code_example", "Step 1 — Set a Safe Root, Then Raise Your Package",
          "```properties\n# application.properties\n# Conservative default for ALL loggers (including libraries)\nlogging.level.root=INFO\n\n# Your own code, more verbose while investigating\nlogging.level.com.bookstore=DEBUG\n\n# A single noisy/important class\nlogging.level.com.bookstore.order.OrderService=TRACE\n\n# Quiet the framework\nlogging.level.org.springframework.web=WARN\nlogging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=WARN\n```\n\nChild loggers inherit the parent's level unless overridden, so one package line covers all its subpackages."),
        S("step", "Step 2 — YAML and Per-Environment Profiles",
          "```yaml\n# application-dev.yml  (active with spring.profiles.active=dev)\nlogging:\n  level:\n    root: INFO\n    com.bookstore: DEBUG\n    org.springframework: WARN\n```\n\nUse `application-dev.yml`, `application-prod.yml` profiles so dev can run DEBUG while production stays at INFO/WARN — no manual edits at deploy time."),
        S("step", "Step 3 — Change Levels at Runtime with Actuator",
          "```bash\n# Requires the actuator dependency and the loggers endpoint exposed\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8080/actuator/loggers/com.bookstore.order \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"configuredLevel\": \"DEBUG\"}'\n```\n\nDuring an incident you can switch a package to DEBUG, reproduce the issue, then set it back — all without restarting or redeploying."),
        S("common_mistakes", "Common Mistakes",
          "- **`logging.level.root=DEBUG`**: turns on DEBUG for every dependency, producing thousands of framework lines that bury your own logs.\n- **Committing DEBUG to prod**: leaving your package at DEBUG after local troubleshooting, hurting performance and inflating log storage bills.\n- **One properties file for all envs**: not using profiles, so you can't have different levels per environment without editing files."),
        S("key_points", "Key Points",
          "- **`logging.level.<package>=LEVEL`** in application.properties/yml controls a package and its children.\n- **Keep `root=INFO`**, raise only your own packages — never set root to DEBUG.\n- **Target a single class** with its fully-qualified name.\n- **Profiles** (`application-{profile}`) keep dev verbose and prod quiet.\n- **Actuator `/actuator/loggers`** changes levels at runtime, no restart."),
        S("speakable_answer", "How to Answer This Verbally",
          "In Spring Boot you configure different log levels per package in application.properties or application.yml using the logging.level prefix. The strategy is to keep the root logger at a conservative level like INFO, and then raise just the packages you care about. So I'd set logging.level.root equals INFO as the baseline for everything, including all the libraries, and then add logging.level.com.mycompany.myapp equals DEBUG to get detailed logging only for my own code. The logger name matches the Java package structure, so that line covers every class in that package and its subpackages, and child packages inherit the parent's level unless I override them. I can go even more granular and set a single class, like logging.level.com.myapp.OrderService equals TRACE, for a targeted deep dive. A common pattern is quieting the framework by setting org.springframework and org.hibernate to WARN, because their DEBUG output is overwhelming. The reason you don't just set root to DEBUG is that it switches on DEBUG for Spring, Hibernate, Tomcat, everything — thousands of lines per request that bury your own logs. For different environments I'd use Spring profiles, so application-dev has DEBUG and application-prod stays at INFO. The thing that really stands out to interviewers is mentioning Spring Boot Actuator. If Actuator is enabled, you can change a log level at runtime with a POST to the slash actuator slash loggers endpoint — no restart needed. During a production incident that's genuinely useful: flip a package to DEBUG, reproduce the bug, then flip it back."),
    ],
    hook="The secret to readable Spring Boot logs is one rule — keep the root logger quiet and only turn the volume up on your own package.",
)

