U["jbf-debug-log-levels-004"] = dict(
    layout_type="concept-explainer",
    direct_answer="The log level hierarchy is **inclusive**: the configured level is a minimum threshold, so it shows that level and everything more severe. Setting **INFO** shows INFO, WARN, and ERROR while suppressing DEBUG and TRACE. Loggers also inherit: a child package logger uses its parent's level unless explicitly overridden.",
    interviewer_intent={
        "testing": "Whether you understand level filtering as an inclusive threshold and know about logger (package) inheritance.",
        "common_mistake": "Believing INFO shows only INFO, or getting the severity direction backwards so ERROR seems to show more output.",
        "to_stand_out": "Explain logger inheritance through the package hierarchy and note that guarded/parameterized logging avoids the cost of suppressed statements.",
    },
    sections=[
        S("overview", "How the Threshold Works",
          "The level hierarchy is an **inclusive filter**. When you set a logger to INFO, you're saying \"show me INFO and anything more severe than INFO\" — so INFO, WARN, and ERROR appear, while DEBUG and TRACE are dropped.\n\nThe five levels in increasing severity are `TRACE < DEBUG < INFO < WARN < ERROR`. Setting any level lets that level and everything to its right pass. Lower the threshold to see more detail; raise it to see only the serious stuff."),
        S("concept_map", "Threshold Behaviour",
          "blue|Set DEBUG|~Very verbose|DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR|TRACE hidden|local development\nemerald|Set INFO|~Production default|INFO, WARN, ERROR|DEBUG/TRACE hidden|business + problems\namber|Set WARN|~Quiet|WARN, ERROR only|INFO hidden|just the surprises\nrose|Set ERROR|~Silent|ERROR only|everything else hidden|failures only"),
        S("code_example", "Same Code, Different Thresholds",
          "```java\nlog.trace(\"entry: registerCustomer({})\", email); // shown only at TRACE\nlog.debug(\"validating email {}\", email);          // shown at DEBUG and below threshold\nlog.info(\"registering customer {}\", email);        // shown at INFO, WARN... no, at INFO threshold\nlog.warn(\"duplicate attempt for {}\", email);       // shown at INFO and WARN thresholds\nlog.error(\"failed to save {}\", email);             // shown at every threshold up to ERROR\n```\n\n```properties\n# logging.level.com.example=INFO\n#   -> trace + debug are suppressed\n#   -> info, warn, error all print\n```\n\nNothing in the code changes; only the configured threshold decides what reaches the output."),
        S("comparison_table", "What Each Setting Shows",
          "| Configured level | Messages emitted |\n|---|---|\n| TRACE | TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR |\n| DEBUG | DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR |\n| INFO | INFO, WARN, ERROR |\n| WARN | WARN, ERROR |\n| ERROR | ERROR only |\n\nThe lower (more verbose) the level, the more rows you get."),
        S("common_mistakes", "Common Mistakes",
          "- **Exclusive thinking**: expecting INFO to show only INFO, then being confused that WARN/ERROR still appear.\n- **Inheritance surprise**: assuming setting `com.app` to DEBUG also lowers `com.app.sub` when that subpackage is explicitly set to INFO — explicit settings win.\n- **Backwards severity**: thinking ERROR is the most verbose; it's the least."),
        S("key_points", "Key Points",
          "- **Inclusive threshold**: a level shows itself and all more-severe levels.\n- **Order**: TRACE < DEBUG < INFO < WARN < ERROR.\n- **INFO** in prod shows business events plus WARN/ERROR; hides DEBUG/TRACE.\n- **Logger inheritance**: child packages inherit the parent level unless overridden.\n- **Use parameterized logging** (`{}`) so suppressed statements stay cheap."),
        S("speakable_answer", "How to Answer This Verbally",
          "The log level hierarchy works inclusively, not exclusively. When you set a logger to INFO, you're not saying show me only INFO messages — you're saying show me INFO and everything more severe than INFO. So INFO, WARN, and ERROR all appear, while DEBUG and TRACE get filtered out. The five levels from least to most severe are TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, and ERROR. Think of the configured level as a minimum threshold: everything at that level or higher in severity passes through. So if you set WARN, you only see WARN and ERROR. If you set ERROR, you only see ERROR. And if you set DEBUG, you see everything except TRACE. This is what makes logging configuration so flexible — you control verbosity with a single setting and no code change. The hierarchy also applies to loggers themselves, through your package structure. If I set com.example to INFO but never configure com.example.payment, that subpackage inherits INFO from its parent. But if I explicitly set com.example.payment to DEBUG, that explicit setting wins for just that package. Two mistakes freshers make: first, thinking INFO literally means only INFO, then being confused when WARN and ERROR still show up. Second, getting the direction backwards — ERROR is more severe than DEBUG, so setting ERROR gives you fewer messages, not more. One more practical note: use parameterized logging with curly-brace placeholders, so that even the suppressed debug statements don't waste time building strings that never get printed."),
    ],
    hook="If you've ever set a logger to INFO and wondered why WARN messages still showed up, the answer is that a level is a floor, not an exact match.",
)

