U["jbf-debug-log-levels-005"] = dict(
    layout_type="concept-explainer",
    direct_answer="**Too much logging** buries the signal in noise, costs CPU/disk I/O, inflates storage bills, and — worst of all — causes **alert fatigue** when everything is ERROR. **Too little logging** leaves you blind: you can't reconstruct what happened during an incident. The goal is **intentional, leveled logging** with business context, not logging everything 'just in case'.",
    interviewer_intent={
        "testing": "Whether you see logging as having real costs and trade-offs, not as a free 'more is better' activity.",
        "common_mistake": "Discussing only one extreme, or assuming logging is costless so adding more is always safe.",
        "to_stand_out": "Name alert fatigue specifically and mention concrete costs — I/O, cloud log ingestion bills, and the inability to diagnose without business identifiers in logs.",
    },
    sections=[
        S("overview", "The Logging Balance Problem",
          "Logging is a balance: too much is as harmful as too little. **Over-logging** creates a signal-to-noise problem — a critical ERROR hides among thousands of DEBUG lines — and it has real costs: disk I/O, network bandwidth to ship logs, storage/retention bills, and CPU to format messages. **Under-logging** leaves you flying blind: when a customer reports a failure, you can't reconstruct what happened.\n\nThe target is **intentional logging**: business events at INFO, surprises at WARN, failures at ERROR, DEBUG off unless actively troubleshooting — with identifiers (order ID, user ID) so events can be correlated."),
        S("concept_map", "Two Failure Modes",
          "rose|Too Much|~Signal lost in noise|I/O + CPU cost|cloud ingestion bills|alert fatigue\nemerald|Just Right|~Leveled + contextual|INFO business events|IDs for correlation|DEBUG off by default\namber|Too Little|~Flying blind|can't diagnose incidents|no audit trail|redeploy just to add logs"),
        S("tradeoffs", "Weighing the Two Extremes",
          "**Too much**: log files grow to gigabytes/day and become unsearchable. Disk I/O can saturate and slow the whole app. Cloud logging (CloudWatch, Datadog) bills by volume, so verbose DEBUG in prod adds real money. And logging every caught exception at ERROR pages on-call for routine events — **alert fatigue** — so real failures get ignored.\n\n**Too little**: a customer says their payment failed, but you only logged successes — now you can't tell if it was a timeout, a validation error, or a DB outage. Post-incident reviews become guesswork, security incidents can't be traced, and you end up redeploying just to add logging while customers are affected."),
        S("code_example", "Noise vs Signal",
          "```java\n// Noise: logs every iteration at INFO, no context\nfor (Item i : cart.items()) {\n    log.info(\"processing item\"); // floods logs, says nothing useful\n}\n\n// Signal: one leveled, contextual line per meaningful event\nlog.debug(\"Pricing {} items for cart {}\", cart.size(), cart.id()); // dev detail\nlog.info(\"Order {} placed: {} items, total {}\",\n         order.id(), order.size(), order.total());                 // business event w/ IDs\n```\n\nThe second style is searchable, correlatable by `order.id()`, and stays quiet at production INFO level."),
        S("common_mistakes", "Common Mistakes",
          "- **Logging every variable**: leaving debug-everything statements in production code, treating logging as free.\n- **ERROR overuse**: logging expected exceptions at ERROR, creating alert storms and desensitizing the team.\n- **No business context**: logging 'operation failed' with no order ID or user ID, so the line is useless during an incident."),
        S("key_points", "Key Points",
          "- **Over-logging** = noise + I/O cost + storage bills + alert fatigue.\n- **Under-logging** = blind during incidents, forced redeploys to add logs.\n- **Logging isn't free**: CPU to format, I/O to write, network to ship, money to store.\n- **Add identifiers** (order/user/request ID) so events correlate.\n- **Right-size**: INFO for events, WARN for surprises, ERROR for failures, DEBUG off by default."),
        S("speakable_answer", "How to Answer This Verbally",
          "Logging too much and too little both cause serious problems, just different ones. Start with too much. The immediate issue is noise — if you log every method call and every variable at DEBUG in production, the logs become unreadable, and when a real error happens it's buried in thousands of routine lines. There's also a performance cost, because writing logs is disk I/O and disk is slow; at high volume you can actually saturate I/O and slow the whole application down. Then there's money — cloud logging services charge by data volume, so shipping verbose DEBUG logs to something like CloudWatch or Datadog can add real cost to the monthly bill. But the worst consequence is alert fatigue: if you log every caught exception at ERROR, including things like validation failures, your on-call team gets paged constantly, they start ignoring alerts, and a genuine database outage gets missed. On the flip side, too little logging leaves you blind. If a customer says their payment failed but you only logged successful payments, you have no idea what went wrong — was it a timeout, a validation error, a database failure? You end up deploying new code just to add logging, which stretches incident resolution from minutes to hours while customers are affected. The right balance is intentional logging: business events at INFO, handled surprises at WARN, real failures at ERROR, and DEBUG off in production unless you're actively troubleshooting. And always include context like order IDs and user IDs so you can correlate events. The thing that impresses interviewers is acknowledging that logging has real costs — CPU to format, I/O to write, network to ship, storage to keep. Treating it as expensive makes you more deliberate about what you log."),
    ],
    hook="Logging feels free, so people add more and more — until a real outage hides inside a million harmless lines and nobody notices.",
)

